# Tracer: an interned template instance may be reused across nested checked
# environments without becoming a duplicate declaration.
#
# Before: larger theorem and case-proof consumers could collect the same
# materialized template object in separate well-definedness children. Merging
# those children rejected the second copy solely because its canonical name was
# already present, even though both copies owned the exact same SymbolId, role,
# and identifier kind.
#
# Now: exact-identity declaration commits are idempotent. The template-backed
# set is checked in the proposition body, theorem preflight, explicit
# definition proof, and final theorem conclusion without a name collision.
#
# Boundary: this does not permit two independently declared objects with the
# same spelling. A focused Rust regression keeps distinct SymbolIds rejected.

template<X set>:
    have fn child_reused_set(x X) power_set(X) = {y X: y = y}

prop child_reuse_law(X set, x X):
    forall y X:
        y $in \child_reused_set<X>(x)
        =>:
            y $in \child_reused_set<X>(x)

thm child_reuse_law_is_available:
    ? forall X nonempty_set, x X:
        $child_reuse_law(X, x)
    by def:
        ? $child_reuse_law(X, x)
